<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">American frontier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Old West, Wild West</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The American frontier is the history, geography and culture of the westward wave of American settlement in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, from the European colonies of the early 17th century to the admission of the last contiguous western territories as states in 1912. Encouraged after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a>, it carried the idea of manifest destiny and was spurred by laws such as the Homestead Act of 1862. It was a story of conquest as well as settlement: Indian wars were fought across the West, and the Native population fell from 600,000 in 1800 to as few as 250,000 by 1890, mostly through disease. The frontier myth became a defining part of American national identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-frontier.jpg" alt="American frontier" width="330" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Francis Barry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitting_bull_and_buffalo_bill_c1885.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
